. The grid spans below, at, above, and off those anchors, so the experiment can distinguish \"sensitivity with a minimum at the true period\" (dependence) from \"flat/arbitrary behavior\" (no dependence).\n  - 96 \u2014 daily, the primary natural-period anchor (baseline).\n  - 672 \u2014 weekly, the secondary natural-period anchor.\n  - 24, 48 \u2014 sub-daily values that understate the daily cycle (24 and 48 both divide 96; they test misspecification below the true period).\n  - 192 \u2014 2\u00d7 daily overestimate (tests misspecification above the daily period).\n  - 336 \u2014 0.5\u00d7 weekly (mid-scale overestimate of daily, underestimate of weekly).\n  - 720 \u2014 near-weekly miss (720 \u2248 672+48; also an exact 24-multiple) to probe the sharpness/width of the valley near the weekly anchor.\n  - 200 \u2014 explicit NEGATIVE CONTROL (off-cycle period): 200 is neither a multiple nor a divisor of 96 or 672 (200/96\u22482.08, 672/200=3.36), a genuinely degenerate/off-cycle setting. Under the valley hypothesis it should be among the worst; under \"no dependence\" it should be indistinguishable from 96/672.\nNull hypothesis (formalized): within a fixed pred_len, expected test MSE is identical across all period settings. Alternative: test MSE depends on the period setting, with minimum near a natural period (96 and/or 672).\n\n---\n2. CONTROLS (everything except `period` fixed; seed pairing; split/normalization)\n  - FIXED per model config: input/context length (96, ETT-benchmark default), label_len (if any) at repo default, optimizer/update rule and its hyperparameters (e.g., the repo's default optimizer/lr/weight decay, frozen), loss = MSE during training, batch size, total epochs cap, early-stopping patience (validation loss, patience 3 by default), denormalization of predictions to original scale before computing metrics. These defaults are recorded as part of each run's logged config (hash) so any drift is detectable.\n  - SEED PAIRING (block design): for each (period, horizon) cell, the identical seed set s \u2208 {0,1,2} is used; a per-seed run is a separate training run (a model trained for one pred_len is never reused for another). Within-seed differences MSE(p,h,s) \u2212 MSE(p_natural,h,s) are computed so that seed-to-seed variability, which affects all periods equally, cancels in the paired comparison. RNG fully seeded (python/hash/numpy/torch manual_seed) before each run.\n  - SPLIT: time-based, no shuffle \u2014 ETT convention: train = first 70% of rows, val = next 10%, test = final 20% (temporally ordered).\n  - NORMALIZATION: per-channel z-score computed on the TRAIN segment only, applied to val and test; predictions denormalized with those train statistics before MSE/MAE. This prevents test-set leakage.\n  - MULTI-HORIZON: pred_len \u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720}; each (period, pred_len) is a separate training run; everything else identical.\n  - ENVIRONMENT CONTROL: single repo commit, fixed ETTm1 file (recorded hash), identical hardware for all runs, deterministic flag on.\n\n---\n3. ANALYSIS PLAN\nPrimary outputs per (period, pred_len): mean\u00b1std MSE and MAE over the 3 seeds on the denormalized test set.\n  (a) PERIOD \u00d7 HORIZON TABLE: rows = period {8 settings incl. control}, columns = pred_len {96,192,336,720}, cells = mean\u00b1std MSE (and a companion MAE table).\n  (b) PER-HORIZON BEST PERIOD: argmin of mean MSE per pred_len; check whether the winner is 96 or 672 (vs 200; under the valley hypothesis the winner must never be the off-cycle control) and whether the winner is stable across seeds (same best period for \u22652 of 3 seeds).\n  (c) VALLEY TEST: within-seed paired difference \u0394(p,h,s) = MSE(p,h,s) \u2212 within-seed anchor MSE(96,h,s) (and separately vs 672); average over seeds. Then per horizon test the monotone-in-distance claim: define d(p) = min(|p\u221296|, |p\u2212672|) and report the Spearman rank correlation between d(p) and mean MSE across periods, plus discrete monotonicity readouts (MSE(24) \u2265 MSE(96)?, MSE(192) \u2265 MSE(96)?, MSE(336) \u2265 MSE(672)?, MSE(720) \u2265 MSE(672)?). A negative control that lands mid-table or is best would falsify the valley pattern.\n  (d) WITHIN-SEED PAIRED COMPARISON: per horizon, report mean\u00b1std of \u0394(p,h,s) across the 3 seeds. With n=3 power is low, so report paired differences and sign consistency descriptively; run a Wilcoxon signed-rank test only if seeds are increased (optional extension); explicitly do not over-claim significance from 3 seeds.\n  (e) SEED-STABILITY REPORT: mean\u00b1std per cell; confirm the per-horizon period ranking is preserved across seeds; the dependence claim requires period-driven variation to exceed within-cell seed std.\n  (f) RESULT INTERPRETATION STATEMENT: SUPPORT for \"performance depends on period\" if test MSE varies with period at fixed horizon beyond seed noise AND is minimized at/near 96/672 AND the off-cycle control (200) is never the best AND the valley/monotonicity readouts hold AND the best-period ranking is seed-stable. REFUTE (no material dependence) if MSE is effectively flat across all periods within seed std, or minima land on arbitrary/off-cycle values, or the per-horizon ranking is not reproducible across seeds.\n  (g) SANITY/feasible checks: no NaN/inf in predictions; normalization statistics from train only; determinism holds (Step 0.4); logged config hash identical across all runs except the `period` field.\n\n---\n4. FEASIBILITY (CPU-only bounded budget; estimates only)\n  - Configs: 8 periods \u00d7 4 horizons = 32 unique (period, pred_len) configs \u00d7 3 seeds = 96 training runs.\n  - Per-config cost estimate: with ETTm1 at assumed scale (~70k rows, ~48\u201349k training windows at context 96), batch 32, ~10\u201320 epochs, a small TS model, CPU-only with 224 cores: order 0.5\u20132 h per run for pred_len 96\u2013720 \u2192 roughly 48\u2013192 core-hours for the full grid. With 8\u201332 parallel workers this is roughly 2\u201